[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124511,
      "id": "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799",
      "title": "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7b2dde0e-a441-5396-92ab-a1e3eb5eb2c2",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=5$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "e4ca8977-110d-5777-b88d-c6c118525885",
