    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cf7b641a-e4d0-57ce-bcf4-dbfd0d4ce68e",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A)<4$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
